{"title":"Twitch’s money guy talks about the revenue split controversy and its monetization long game (TechCrunch, 2023-10-26T22:28:10+00:00)","text":"Twitch’s money guy talks about the revenue split controversy and its monetization long game (TechCrunch, 2023-10-26T22:28:10+00:00)
I have to wonder if those decisions to change these policies were in response to competition from other platforms.

I would say it this way: I think they are an acknowledgement, and maybe a closer connectivity to the community via Dan specifically. I credit Dan in his short time as CEO to really make some big, hard decisions. Both the launch of Partner Plus, as well as simulcasting, the embed stuff we talked about in the keynote — these are three things that I think are very much an acknowledgement of the feedback from the community. We’ve made that change, so I wouldn’t call it out of competitive pressures. I would call it out of focus on the community.","entities":["TechCrunch","competition","revenue split controversy","monetization","Twitch’s money guy talks about the revenue split controversy and its monetization long game","community feedback","Partner Plus","Dan","simulcasting","Twitch"],"facts":["SBF’s trial starts soon, but how did he — and FTX — get here? -PUBLISHED_BY-> TechCrunch","Sam Altman backs teens’ startup, Google unveils the Pixel 8 and TikTok tests an ad-free tier -PUBLISHED_BY-> TechCrunch","More from the US v Google trial: vertical search, pre-installs and the case of Firefox/Yahoo -PUBLISHED_BY-> TechCrunch","After warning, the EU opens investigation into X over alleged disinformation -PUBLISHED_BY-> TechCrunch","SBF Trial: The latest updates from the FTX collapse’s courtroom drama -PUBLISHED_BY-> TechCrunch"]}

{"title":"The jury finally hears from Sam Bankman-Fried (The Verge, 2023-10-28T00:12:41+00:00)","text":"The jury finally hears from Sam Bankman-Fried (The Verge, 2023-10-28T00:12:41+00:00)
When he found it, he was “very surprised!”

Besides the two obvious lies Bankman-Fried told on the stand — about Alameda Research’s name and about “allow_negative” — I have been struck by how little he seems to know about his own companies. Apparently, Singh, Wang, and Ellison were out there just doing whatever their little hearts desired. Because Bankman-Fried was a CEO, but definitely not the kind that pays any attention to money at his crypto trading firm and futures exchange.","entities":["The jury finally hears from Sam Bankman-Fried","Alameda Research","allow_negative","Ellison","Singh","Wang","Sam Bankman-Fried","The Verge"],"facts":["The jury finally hears from Sam Bankman-Fried -MENTIONS-> Gary Wang","The jury finally hears from Sam Bankman-Fried -MENTIONS-> Caroline Ellison","The jury finally hears from Sam Bankman-Fried -MENTIONS-> Mark Cohen","The jury finally hears from Sam Bankman-Fried -MENTIONS-> Adam Yedidia","The jury finally hears from Sam Bankman-Fried -MENTIONS-> Securities and Exchange Commission"]}

{"title":"The FTX trial is bigger than Sam Bankman-Fried (The Verge, 2023-09-28T12:00:00+00:00)","text":"The FTX trial is bigger than Sam Bankman-Fried (The Verge, 2023-09-28T12:00:00+00:00)
If it seems like his peer group supported him, or worse, fawned over him, that’s a real problem for the industry’s reputation.”

After FTX declared bankruptcy, Bankman-Fried went on an extensive media tour, doing interviews with The New York Times’ Andrew Ross Sorkin and Good Morning America. Even under house arrest, he did interviews, including with The New Yorker. Bankman-Fried seemed to think that if he just explained things, everyone would understand that what he did was a mistake, not a crime.","entities":["The New York Times","FTX trial","The FTX trial is bigger than Sam Bankman-Fried","extensive media tour","interviews","Andrew Ross Sorkin","Good Morning America","The New Yorker","FTX","Sam Bankman-Fried","The Verge"],"facts":["He’s Hockey’s Brightest Young Star. This Is What Makes His Shot So Special. -PUBLISHED_BY-> The New York Times","How U.S. Women’s Soccer Team Lured Emma Hayes Away From Chelsea -PUBLISHED_BY-> The New York Times","After a Stunning Snub, College Football Is Facing an Identity Crisis -PUBLISHED_BY-> The New York Times","N.F.L. Executives on the Biggest Questions Facing Super Bowl Contenders -PUBLISHED_BY-> The New York Times","Which M.L.B. Playoff Team Has the Most Daunting Home Field? We Ranked Them. -PUBLISHED_BY-> The New York Times"]}

{"title":"In the end, the FTX trial was about the friends screwed along the way (The Verge, 2023-10-26T12:00:00+00:00)","text":"In the end, the FTX trial was about the friends screwed along the way (The Verge, 2023-10-26T12:00:00+00:00)
In it, Bankman-Fried tried to justify the missing funds through the margin loan program. It was such a flimsy excuse that even George Stephanopoulos could see through it.

Did Bankman-Fried really think he could get away with it all? He must still think so, or he would not be dragging his closest companions’ names through the mud in a jury trial. Throughout the entire prosecution’s case, the defense has struggled to shake the credibility of witnesses such as Yedidia, Wang, and Ellison. The hits the defense made on Singh didn’t make me doubt the overall truth of his story — they just made me think that Singh was kind of self-serving and trying to secure himself a lenient sentence.","entities":["Bankman-Fried","In the end, the FTX trial was about the friends screwed along the way","Ellison","Singh","missing funds","FTX trial","margin loan program","George Stephanopoulos","the defense","FTX","Wang","Yedidia","the prosecution","The Verge"],"facts":["Sam Altman backs teens’ startup, Google unveils the Pixel 8 and TikTok tests an ad-free tier -MENTIONS-> Bankman-Fried","SBF’s trial starts soon, but how did he — and FTX — get here? -MENTIONS-> Bankman-Fried","Sex, Signal messages, and sabotaging FTX's code: SBF execs and Bahamas roommates tell all in court -MENTIONS-> Bankman-Fried","Today the FTX jury suffered through a code review -MENTIONS-> Bankman-Fried","Bankman-Fried -SAID-> Alameda Research"]}

{"title":"The people who ruined the internet (The Verge, 2023-11-01T13:00:00+00:00)","text":"The people who ruined the internet (The Verge, 2023-11-01T13:00:00+00:00)
In the beginning, the internet was seen as an improvement on the spirit of the public library. Here was an opportunity to transcend the gatekeepers controlling who could publish a book, allowing mankind to fully connect and share knowledge. Instead, we have ended up in a situation arguably worse than before, where nearly all online information runs through a single company, which assumes a veneer of civic utility, of impassive authority, when it is very much not a neutral entity.

“There were so many true believers at Google in the early days,” Cutts told me. “As companies get big, it gets harder to get things done. Inevitably, people start to think about profit or quarterly numbers.” He claimed that, at least while he was there, search quality always came before financial goals, but he believes that the public underestimates how Google is shaping what they see, saying, “I deeply, deeply, deeply believe search engines are newspaper-like entities, making editorial decisions.”","entities":["internet","The people who ruined the internet","Cutts","search engines","public library","Google","The Verge"],"facts":["How OpenAI's ChatGPT has changed the world in just a year -MENTIONS-> internet","ChatGPT can now browse the internet (again) -MENTIONS-> internet","Gen Z just saw the worst war in Israel in 50 years break out all over the internet, but they were raised on 'disturbing images' -MENTIONS-> internet","6 VCs explain how startups can capture and defend marketshare in the AI era -MENTIONS-> internet","California’s privacy watchdog eyes AI rules with opt-out and access rights -MENTIONS-> internet"]}

{"title":"Beeper reversed engineered iMessage to bring blue bubble texts to Android users (TechCrunch, 2023-12-05T15:00:57+00:00)","text":"Beeper reversed engineered iMessage to bring blue bubble texts to Android users (TechCrunch, 2023-12-05T15:00:57+00:00)
The push to bring iMessage to Android users today adds a new contender. A startup called Beeper, which had been working on a multi-platform messaging aggregator, is now launching a new app called Beeper Mini which will allow Android users to send and receive end-to-end encrypted iMessage chats for just $1.99 per month. Beeper Mini was made possible because the team behind the app has managed to reverse-engineer the iMessage protocol, they say.

“That’s the big breakthrough,” explains Beeper co-founder and CEO Eric Migicovsky, previously the founder of smartwatch startup Pebble. “We’re not actually a middleman anymore. The research that we’ve done is actually reverse-engineering the iMessage protocol, down to the lowest layer of the protocol. So Beeper Mini doesn’t use a Mac server as a relay like all the other apps — they have a Mac Mini in a data center somewhere.","entities":["Android","Mac mini","TechCrunch","Beeper","Beeper Mini","iMessage protocol","team behind the app","Eric Migicovsky","Beeper reversed engineered iMessage to bring blue bubble texts to Android users","iMessage","Pebble"],"facts":["What to know about Fortnite maker Epic Games’ antitrust battle with Google, starting today -MENTIONS-> Android","ChatGPT’s mobile app hits record $4.58M in revenue in September -MENTIONS-> Android","ChatGPT: Everything you need to know about the AI-powered chatbot -MENTIONS-> Android","The best Black Friday deals you can get for under $25 -MENTIONS-> Android","More from the US v Google trial: vertical search, pre-installs and the case of Firefox/Yahoo -MENTIONS-> Android"]}

{"title":"Beeper Mini is back in operation after Apple’s attempt to shut it down (TechCrunch, 2023-12-11T18:08:48+00:00)","text":"Beeper Mini is back in operation after Apple’s attempt to shut it down (TechCrunch, 2023-12-11T18:08:48+00:00)
Beeper Mini, the app bringing blue bubble iMessage texts to Android users, is back in operation on Monday after a long weekend that saw Apple putting an end to Beeper’s services, claiming it was a security risk. Now, the startup behind the new app says it has resumed functioning, but hasn’t disclosed how that’s been made possible. In addition, Beeper Mini will be free for the time being given the unstable nature of Beeper’s status and Apple’s attempts at shutting it down.

Founded by former Pebble smartwatch founder Eric Migicovsky, Beeper had originally been working on an app that aggregated all of a user’s chat apps into a single interface. However, upon the discovery of new technology that allowed it to reverse-engineer the iMessage protocol, it set out to build a new app called Beeper Mini that solely focused on bringing iMessage chats to Android users for $1.99 per month. That included support for high-quality photos and videos, tapback reactions, typing indicators, read receipts and more.","entities":["Android","Apple","TechCrunch","Beeper Mini is back in operation after Apple’s attempt to shut it down","Beeper","Beeper Mini","Eric Migicovsky","iMessage","Pebble"],"facts":["What to know about Fortnite maker Epic Games’ antitrust battle with Google, starting today -MENTIONS-> Android","ChatGPT’s mobile app hits record $4.58M in revenue in September -MENTIONS-> Android","ChatGPT: Everything you need to know about the AI-powered chatbot -MENTIONS-> Android","The best Black Friday deals you can get for under $25 -MENTIONS-> Android","More from the US v Google trial: vertical search, pre-installs and the case of Firefox/Yahoo -MENTIONS-> Android"]}

{"title":"How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence (The Age, 2023-12-09T18:30:00+00:00)","text":"How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence (The Age, 2023-12-09T18:30:00+00:00)
AI was once laughed off. Now the richest men in Silicon Valley were shelling out billions to keep from being left behind. The lost ethics board

When Musk invested in DeepMind, he broke his own informal rule – that he would not invest in any company he didn’t run himself. The downsides of his decision were already apparent when, only a month or so after his birthday spat with Page, he again found himself face to face with his former friend and fellow billionaire. Larry Page, co-founder of Google. Credit: Bloomberg The occasion was the first meeting of DeepMind’s ethics board, on August 14, 2015. The board had been set up at the insistence of the startup’s founders to ensure that their technology did no harm after the sale. The members convened in a conference room just outside Musk’s office at SpaceX. But that’s where Musk’s control ended. When Google bought DeepMind, it bought the whole thing. Musk was out.","entities":["Musk","SpaceX","How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence","Larry Page","DeepMind ethics board","first meeting of DeepMind’s ethics board","birthday spat","DeepMind","The Age","Google","Silicon Valley","artificial intelligence","Bloomberg"],"facts":["Why Mozilla is betting on a decentralized social networking future -MENTIONS-> Musk","After warning, the EU opens investigation into X over alleged disinformation -MENTIONS-> Musk","Founders, are events useful? -MENTIONS-> Musk","X (formerly Twitter) is worst for disinformation, per EU analysis -MENTIONS-> Musk","The UK just laid out new rules for the internet — it only gets harder from here -MENTIONS-> Musk"]}

{"title":"How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence (The Age, 2023-12-09T18:30:00+00:00)","text":"How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence (The Age, 2023-12-09T18:30:00+00:00)
Researchers inside OpenAI, Google and other companies thought this rapidly improving technology could be a path to AGI. But Amodei was unhappy about the Microsoft deal because he thought it was taking OpenAI in a really commercial direction. He and other researchers went to the board to try to push Altman out, according to five people familiar with the matter. After they failed, they left. Like DeepMind’s founders before them, they worried that their new corporate overlords would favour commercial interests over safety.

In 2021, the group of about 15 engineers and scientists created a new lab called Anthropic. The plan was to build AI the way the effective altruists thought it should done – with very tight controls. “There was no attempt to remove Sam Altman from OpenAI by the co-founders of Anthropic,” said an Anthropic spokesperson, Sally Aldous.","entities":["How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence","effective altruists","Amodei","Microsoft deal","commercial interests","Sally Aldous","group of about 15 engineers and scientists","creation of Anthropic","attempt to remove Sam Altman from OpenAI","DeepMind","Microsoft","The Age","Google","safety","Anthropic"],"facts":["How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence -MENTIONS-> disinformation","How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence -MENTIONS-> Musk","How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence -MENTIONS-> Page","How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence -MENTIONS-> Mark Zuckerberg","How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence -MENTIONS-> Peter Thiel"]}

{"title":"How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence (The Age, 2023-12-09T18:30:00+00:00)","text":"How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence (The Age, 2023-12-09T18:30:00+00:00)
They believed that AI could find a cure for cancer or solve climate change, but they worried that AI bots might do things their creators had not intended. If the machines became more intelligent than humans, the Rationalists argued, the machines could turn on their creators. Thiel had become enormously wealthy through an early investment in Facebook and through his work with Musk in the early days of PayPal. He had developed a fascination with the singularity, a trope of science fiction that describes the moment when intelligent technology can no longer be controlled by humanity.

With funding from Thiel, Yudkowsky had expanded his AI lab and created an annual conference on the singularity. Years before, one of Hassabis’ two colleagues had met Yudkowsky, and he snagged them speaking spots at the conference, ensuring they’d be invited to Thiel’s party. Yudkowsky introduced Hassabis to Thiel. Hassabis assumed that lots of people at the party would be trying to squeeze their host for money.","entities":["Musk","Thiel","Rationalists","Yudkowsky","annual conference on the singularity","Thiel’s party","singularity","Hassabis","How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence","AI lab","PayPal","The Age","Facebook","science fiction","cancer"],"facts":["Why Mozilla is betting on a decentralized social networking future -MENTIONS-> Musk","After warning, the EU opens investigation into X over alleged disinformation -MENTIONS-> Musk","Founders, are events useful? -MENTIONS-> Musk","X (formerly Twitter) is worst for disinformation, per EU analysis -MENTIONS-> Musk","The UK just laid out new rules for the internet — it only gets harder from here -MENTIONS-> Musk"]}

